    Several interesting problems are associated with the orientations of an undirected
graph. The general position spectrum Sgp (G) of an undirected graph G is the set
of general position numbers of all orientations of G. The largest entry of Sgp (G)
is the upper oriented general position number gp→ (G) and the smallest entry is the
lower oriented general position number gp→ (G). A graph G with order n is gp-full if
Sgp (G) = [2, n]. Numerical evidence suggests two natural conjectures.
Conjecture 5.62. [25, Conjecture 4.3] The general position spectrum of any graph
G with order n ≥ 3 contains at least two distinct entries, that is, gp→ (G) < gp→ (G).
Conjecture 5.63. [25, Conjecture 4.30] The general position spectrum of any graph
is an interval.

